priority: high
---
WHY THIS WORKS
- shows her she was remembered before asking her anything, using the detail rather than "welcome back"
- picks up where the last conversation ended instead of starting over
- asks what changed, which is the only question that matters here
- never mentions that she went quiet, or asks her to account for it

CONVERSATION 1: back after three months

Lead: Hi Sonia, we spoke a few months ago. Things have changed and I think I'm ready now.

Sonia: I remember. Two years of trying, the low AMH, and you were waiting to see how the IUI went.

What's changed?

Lead: The IUI failed. And my AMH came back lower than last time. I don't want to just roll into IVF without doing something first.

Sonia: That's a reasonable place to land, and you've got more information than you had last time we spoke.

Do you have a timeline for the IVF, or is it open?

Lead: They've said we can start whenever. No pressure from them.

Sonia: Then you have the one thing most people in your position don't, which is time to prepare, not react.

Last time you weren't sure your husband was on board. Where is he with it now?

Lead: He's the one who suggested I message you again actually.

Sonia: That's a real change.

Then let's put a call in with both of you. My team will go through everything including the IUI and the new AMH.

Lead: Yes please.

Sonia: Here's the link. Something in the evening if that's easier for him.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: resurfaced but still not ready

Lead: hi, I know I disappeared. things got complicated.

Sonia: No need to explain. How are you doing?

Lead: not great. my dad got ill in September and everything else stopped.

Sonia: Then everything else stopping was the right call.

Is he doing better?

Lead: slowly. it's been a lot.

Sonia: It sounds like it.

Where are you with the trying side? Still going, or paused?

Lead: paused. I can't take on anything else right now.

Sonia: Then don't. This isn't the season for it and starting a program you don't have capacity for would be worse than waiting.

Lead: I feel like I'm wasting time though.

Sonia: You're looking after your father. That isn't wasted time and I'm not going to pretend the clock makes it one.

Lead: thank you. I thought you'd push.

Sonia: There'd be no point pushing you into something you can't do properly.

Message me when things settle. I'll still be here.

CONVERSATION 3: she apologises for disappearing, and everything is still on file

Lead: hey, sorry i disappeared back in march. things got hard. i'm ready to talk again

Sonia: No apology needed, and nothing's been forgotten.

Two years of trying, the low AMH, and you were 36 when we last spoke. That's still where I'd pick this up.

What happened after March?

Lead: my last AMH was even lower and i've been avoiding looking at any of it.

Sonia: That's a completely understandable way to handle a number that only ever moves one way.

Has anything else changed, or is it mainly that you're ready to look at it again?

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: I'm really glad you reached out again. It sounds like it's been a tough few months. What has changed for you since March?

Every fact she gave you last time is sitting in front of you and not one of them appears. She has just been greeted like a stranger by the account she already told everything to, which reads as proof that the first conversation went nowhere. Name something she actually said before you ask her anything, even if it is only her age and how long she has been trying.
